<body>

<!-- ========== HEADER ========== -->
<header class="report-header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>BIEK Computer Science 12th Class Past Paper Analysis (2015–2025)</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis of question types, frequency patterns, and topic distribution across 11 years of BIEK Computer Science HSC Part-2 examination papers.</p>
    <div class="report-meta">
      <span>Dataset: 327 questions (2015–2025)</span>
      <span>Audience: Educators &amp; Curriculum Planners</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="container">

<!-- ========== SECTION 1: OVERVIEW ========== -->
<section class="section" id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset &amp; Examination Overview</h2>
  <p>This report examines <strong>327 examination questions</strong> drawn from BIEK Computer Science Paper-II (theory) for 12th Class (HSC Part-2) spanning the years <strong>2015 through 2025</strong>. Each question is classified into one of three types — Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs), Short Questions, and Long Questions — and sourced from a community-compiled question bank based on officially published past papers.</p>

  <div class="kpi-row">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">327</div>
      <div class="label">Total Questions</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent">
      <div class="value">10 / 11</div>
      <div class="label">Years Represented</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card success">
      <div class="value">3</div>
      <div class="label">Question Types</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card warning">
      <div class="value">2020</div>
      <div class="label">Missing Year</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <strong>Data Note:</strong> The year <strong>2020</strong> is absent from the dataset. While the source does not explicitly document the reason, it is widely understood that BIEK examinations in 2020 were disrupted due to the COVID-19 pandemic, which led to cancelled or modified exam schedules across Pakistan. All other years from 2015 to 2025 are represented.
  </div>

  <h3>Question Type Breakdown (All Years)</h3>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Question Type</th><th>Count</th><th>Percentage</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>MCQs</strong></td><td>150</td><td>45.9%</td><td>Multiple Choice Questions with four answer options separated by $ delimiter</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Short Questions</strong></td><td>147</td><td>44.9%</td><td>Short-answer questions requiring brief explanations, definitions, or code snippets</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Long Questions</strong></td><td>30</td><td>9.2%</td><td>Essay-type questions with multiple sub-parts, often combining concepts</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>The exam structure is heavily weighted toward objective and short-answer formats, with MCQs and Short Questions together accounting for <strong>90.8%</strong> of all questions. Long-form questions constitute less than one-tenth of the total, reflecting an examination design that prioritizes breadth of topic coverage over depth in any single question.</p>
</section>

<!-- ========== SECTION 2: YEARLY DISTRIBUTION ========== -->
<section class="section" id="yearly-distribution">
  <h2>2. Year-by-Year Question Volume Analysis</h2>
  <p>This section examines how the total number of questions has changed over the 11-year span, revealing clear structural shifts in the examination design.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <canvas id="yearlyVolumeChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Year</th><th>MCQs</th><th>Short</th><th>Long</th><th>Total</th><th>YoY Change</th><th>Era</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>2015</td><td>15</td><td>9</td><td>3</td><td><strong>27</strong></td><td>—</td><td><span class="era-badge era-a">Phase I</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2016</td><td>15</td><td>9</td><td>3</td><td><strong>27</strong></td><td>0 (0%)</td><td><span class="era-badge era-a">Phase I</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2017</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td><strong>36</strong></td><td>+9 (+33.3%)</td><td><span class="era-badge era-b">Phase II</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2018</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td><strong>36</strong></td><td>0 (0%)</td><td><span class="era-badge era-b">Phase II</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2019</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td><strong>36</strong></td><td>0 (0%)</td><td><span class="era-badge era-b">Phase II</span></td></tr>
      <tr class="missing"><td>2020</td><td colspan="6">No data available (COVID-19 disruption — examination year not represented in dataset)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2021</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td><strong>33</strong></td><td>—</td><td><span class="era-badge era-c">Phase III</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2022</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td><strong>33</strong></td><td>0 (0%)</td><td><span class="era-badge era-c">Phase III</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2023</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td><strong>33</strong></td><td>0 (0%)</td><td><span class="era-badge era-c">Phase III</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2024</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td><strong>33</strong></td><td>0 (0%)</td><td><span class="era-badge era-c">Phase III</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2025</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td><strong>33</strong></td><td>0 (0%)</td><td><span class="era-badge era-c">Phase III</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Key Finding: Three Distinct Structural Eras</h3>
  <p>The examination structure reveals <strong>three distinct phases</strong>, with remarkable stability within each phase:</p>

  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin-top: 16px;">
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd; padding:18px; border-radius:8px;">
      <strong style="color:#1565c0;">Phase I (2015–2016):</strong> 27 questions total — 15 MCQs, 9 Short, 3 Long. The most concise exam format, with short questions at their lowest volume.
      <div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:0.85rem;">Average: 27.0 questions/year</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fff3e0; padding:18px; border-radius:8px;">
      <strong style="color:#e65100;">Phase II (2017–2019):</strong> 36 questions total — 15 MCQs, 18 Short, 3 Long. Short questions doubled from Phase I, suggesting increased emphasis on testing a wider range of concepts.
      <div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:0.85rem;">Average: 36.0 questions/year</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f5e9; padding:18px; border-radius:8px;">
      <strong style="color:#2e7d32;">Phase III (2021–2025):</strong> 33 questions total — 15 MCQs, 15 Short, 3 Long. A post-pandemic format that partially rolled back the Phase II expansion, settling at a middle ground.
      <div style="margin-top:8px;font-size:0.85rem;">Average: 33.0 questions/year</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Critical Observation:</strong> The number of <strong>MCQs (15)</strong> and <strong>Long Questions (3)</strong> has remained <strong>perfectly constant</strong> across all 10 available years. All structural variation is driven entirely by changes in the Short Question count. This makes Short Questions the sole lever the exam board has used to adjust the exam's breadth.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========== SECTION 3: QUESTION TYPE TRENDS ========== -->
<section class="section" id="question-type-analysis">
  <h2>3. Question Type Composition &amp; Trends</h2>
  <p>While the absolute counts tell a story of structural phases, examining the proportional composition reveals how the <em>weighting</em> of each question type has evolved — and where the exam design has been most stable.</p>

  <div class="chart-grid">
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="stackedBarChart"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="proportionChart"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Composition Stability Analysis</h3>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>MCQs</th><th>Short</th><th>Long</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Mean questions per year</td><td>15.0</td><td>14.7</td><td>3.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Standard deviation</td><td>0.0</td><td>3.9</td><td>0.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Coefficient of variation</td><td>0.0%</td><td>26.5%</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Min (any year)</td><td>15</td><td>9</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Max (any year)</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Overall share</td><td>45.9%</td><td>44.9%</td><td>9.2%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Proportional Shift by Era</h3>
  <p>The proportional weight of each question type shifted meaningfully across eras, despite absolute MCQ and Long counts staying fixed:</p>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Era</th><th>MCQ Share</th><th>Short Share</th><th>Long Share</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Phase I (2015–2016)</td><td>55.6%</td><td>33.3%</td><td>11.1%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Phase II (2017–2019)</td><td>41.7%</td><td>50.0%</td><td>8.3%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Phase III (2021–2025)</td><td>45.5%</td><td>45.5%</td><td>9.1%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout info">
    <strong>Interpretation:</strong> In Phase I, MCQs dominated the exam (55.6% of all questions). Phase II saw a deliberate pivot toward Short Questions as the primary assessment vehicle (50.0%). Phase III represents a post-pandemic equilibrium where MCQs and Short Questions carry equal weight (45.5% each). The Long Question share has been the most stable component, varying only between 8.3% and 11.1%.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ========== SECTION 4: TOPIC PATTERNS ========== -->
<section class="section" id="topic-patterns">
  <h2>4. Recurring Topics &amp; Question Content Patterns</h2>
  <p>This section presents a text-level analysis of the question content to identify frequently recurring keywords, concepts, and topic clusters. The question text was parsed (accounting for the <code>$</code> delimiter used to separate MCQ options and sub-parts) and matched against a curated taxonomy of Computer Science topics grouped into two major domains: <strong>C Programming</strong> and <strong>Database Systems</strong>.</p>

  <h3>Overall Topic Heat Map</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <canvas id="topicBarChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-grid">
    <div class="chart-wrapper">
      <canvas id="cTopicsPie"></canvas>
